This PR is a request to support parameters that are on multiple gpus in clip_grad_norm_. I recently did something similar to:
class MyModel(nn.Module):
    def __init__(self, split_gpus):
        self.large_submodule1 = ...
        self.large_submodule2 = ...
        self.split_gpus = split_gpus
    if split_gpus:
        self.large_submodule1.cuda(0)
        self.large_submodule1.cuda(1)

    def forward(self, x):
        x = self.large_submodule1(x)
        if split_gpus:
            x = x.cuda(1) # P2P GPU transfer
        return self.large_submodule2(x)

(ref: https://discuss.pytorch.org/t/model-parallelism-in-pytorch-for-large-r-than-1-gpu-models/778)
and ran into issues when I tried clip_grad_norm_(MyModel.paramaters(), clip) --> I was getting the following error message

arguments are located on different GPUs

As a fix:
I have done the following in clip_grad.py:
device_p = p.get_device()
        try:
            if total_norm.is_cuda and total_norm.get_device() != device_p:
                total_norm = total_norm.cuda(device_p)
       except:
           pass

and
    device_p = p.get_device()
    if clip_coef.is_cuda and clip_coef.get_device() != device_p:
        clip_coef = clip_coef.cuda(device_p)

Thanks!

It would be nice if the context managers that enable/disable autograd could be used a decorator. This would allow one to write code like:
@torch.no_grad()
def test(args):
    pass

In Python 3, this can be trivially implemented with a ContextDecorator, but for Python 2 a solutions should be easy too.
As a workaround I currently use a wrapper in Python 3:
@contextmanager
def no_grad():
    with torch.no_grad():
        yield

which works since contextmanager implements ContextDecorator,

On master:
import torch
@torch.jit.script
def fn():
    a = 1
    b = 2
    c = 3
    d = b * b * c + a - b * a
    return torch.full([1], d)
fn()

RuntimeError:
Expected a 1D LongTensor:
operation failed in interpreter:
@torch.jit.script
def fn():
    a = 1
    b = 2
    c = 3
    d = b * b * c + a - b * a
    return torch.full([1], d)
           ~~~~~~~~~~ <--- HERE

But the following works:
import torch
@torch.jit.script
def fn():
    a = 1
    b = 2
    c = 3
    d = b * c
    return torch.full([1], d)

fn()
